WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.291
<v Examiner>Exam case 10.

2
00:00:02.291 --> 00:00:03.714
<v Examiner>Stroke and the brain.

3
00:00:04.164 --> 00:00:06.964
<v Examiner>You're working in a stroke rehabilitation unit,

4
00:00:06.964 --> 00:00:11.252
<v Examiner>often with people recovering from a stroke, or cerebrovascular accident,

5
00:00:11.252 --> 00:00:15.184
<v Examiner>where parts of the brain were damaged during periods of ischaemia.

6
00:00:15.184 --> 00:00:18.341
<v Examiner>Different areas of the brain control different tasks,

7
00:00:18.341 --> 00:00:23.225
<v Examiner>so the functions a person has lost reflect the part of the brain that was damaged.

8
00:00:23.225 --> 00:00:26.203
<v Examiner>You're working with people with a range of losses,

9
00:00:26.203 --> 00:00:29.837
<v Examiner>and thinking about which parts of the brain must be involved.

10
00:00:30.287 --> 00:00:34.318
Two big questions, seven marks each, on her cerebrum diagram.

11
00:00:34.318 --> 00:00:37.491
Her sessions say the exam uses the same letters.

12
00:00:37.491 --> 00:00:41.060
Then the brain stem questions from her formative test.

13
00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:42.278
<v Examiner>Seven marks.

14
00:00:42.278 --> 00:00:47.462
<v Examiner>Using the image, name the functional area of the cerebrum at each letter, A to G.

15
00:00:47.912 --> 00:00:52.307
The hook, from A to G: plan, speak, move, feel, hear, understand, see.

16
00:00:52.757 --> 00:00:55.063
Letter A, the prefrontal cortex.

17
00:00:55.063 --> 00:00:55.423
Plan.

18
00:00:55.873 --> 00:00:57.254
B, Broca's area.

19
00:00:57.254 --> 00:00:57.772
Speak.

20
00:00:58.222 --> 00:01:01.394
C, the motor area, on the precentral gyrus.

21
00:01:01.394 --> 00:01:01.763
Move.

22
00:01:02.213 --> 00:01:06.184
D, the somatosensory area, on the postcentral gyrus.

23
00:01:06.184 --> 00:01:06.565
Feel.

24
00:01:07.015 --> 00:01:08.583
E, the auditory area.

25
00:01:08.583 --> 00:01:08.957
Hear.

26
00:01:09.407 --> 00:01:10.798
F, Wernicke's area.

27
00:01:10.798 --> 00:01:11.604
Understand.

28
00:01:12.054 --> 00:01:15.047
G, the occipital area, the visual cortex.

29
00:01:15.047 --> 00:01:15.339
See.

30
00:01:16.789 --> 00:01:17.544
<v Examiner>Seven marks.

31
00:01:17.544 --> 00:01:22.635
<v Examiner>For each region, what changes to function or behaviour could follow damage to it?

32
00:01:23.085 --> 00:01:25.303
Lose the area, and you lose its job.

33
00:01:25.753 --> 00:01:29.723
Area A, the prefrontal cortex, does problem-solving, judgement,

34
00:01:29.723 --> 00:01:34.007
holding back inappropriate behaviour, and keeping the mind on goals.

35
00:01:34.007 --> 00:01:39.300
Damage it, and you get poor judgement, inappropriate behaviour, and a loss of focus.

36
00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:44.691
Area B, Broca's area, produces speech and writing that others can understand.

37
00:01:44.691 --> 00:01:48.091
Damage gives difficulty producing speech and writing.

38
00:01:48.541 --> 00:01:54.109
Area C, the motor area, instructs the muscle movements, with every muscle group mapped.

39
00:01:54.109 --> 00:01:58.077
Damage gives a loss of voluntary movement in the mapped parts.

40
00:01:58.527 --> 00:02:03.590
Area D, the somatosensory area, receives touch and the other general senses,

41
00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:05.455
from every part of the body.

42
00:02:05.455 --> 00:02:08.853
Damage gives a loss of touch and general sensation.

43
00:02:09.303 --> 00:02:14.854
Area E, the auditory area, is for hearing, with regions tuned to different frequencies.

44
00:02:14.854 --> 00:02:16.321
Damage affects hearing.

45
00:02:16.771 --> 00:02:21.040
Area F, Wernicke's area, understands spoken and written language.

46
00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:24.323
Damage means the person can't understand language.

47
00:02:24.773 --> 00:02:30.835
Area G, the occipital area, is the visual processing system, the primary visual cortex.

48
00:02:30.835 --> 00:02:32.368
Damage affects vision.

49
00:02:32.818 --> 00:02:33.808
One honest note.

50
00:02:33.808 --> 00:02:35.973
Her deck gives what each area does.

51
00:02:35.973 --> 00:02:40.240
The "damage leads to" half is the podcast's reading of each function.

52
00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:43.271
It's the obvious reading, but it isn't her slide.

53
00:02:44.721 --> 00:02:48.260
Her formative test asks four of these as drop-downs.

54
00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:53.161
Judgement, reasoning and impulse control: area A, the prefrontal cortex.

55
00:02:53.161 --> 00:02:57.312
Producing speech others can understand: area B, Broca's area.

56
00:02:57.312 --> 00:03:01.396
Making sense of sounds: area E, the primary auditory cortex.

57
00:03:01.396 --> 00:03:05.820
Controlling voluntary movement: area C, the primary motor cortex.

58
00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:09.980
<v Examiner>And the brain stem, from her formative test.

59
00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:11.643
<v Examiner>Four people, four injuries.

60
00:03:12.093 --> 00:03:17.961
A person who took no alcohol or drugs behaves like a drunk person after a car accident,

61
00:03:17.961 --> 00:03:21.132
and the blood alcohol test comes back negative.

62
00:03:21.132 --> 00:03:23.357
The damaged part: the cerebellum.

63
00:03:23.807 --> 00:03:28.033
After a head injury, someone breathes with long, gasping in-breaths,

64
00:03:28.033 --> 00:03:31.265
a pause at full inspiration, and a short out-breath.

65
00:03:31.265 --> 00:03:36.671
That's the pons, because the apneustic centre there controls the length of inspiration.

66
00:03:37.121 --> 00:03:41.943
After falling down the stairs, someone breathes very slowly and shallowly,

67
00:03:41.943 --> 00:03:45.657
and responds poorly to high carbon dioxide or low oxygen.

68
00:03:45.657 --> 00:03:51.521
That's the medulla oblongata, because it contains the pacemaker of the respiratory system.

69
00:03:51.971 --> 00:03:57.018
After a motorbike accident, someone's left and right hands act independently.

70
00:03:57.018 --> 00:04:01.606
She can't coordinate tasks that need both hands, like tying shoelaces,

71
00:04:01.606 --> 00:04:05.604
and one hand acts in conflict with the other: the alien hand.

72
00:04:05.604 --> 00:04:07.374
That's the corpus callosum.

73
00:04:08.824 --> 00:04:13.463
Then she asks where each one sits on her diagram, as fill-in-the-blanks.

74
00:04:13.463 --> 00:04:14.622
Here are her keys.

75
00:04:14.622 --> 00:04:18.552
The cerebellum is directly posterior to the fourth ventricle,

76
00:04:18.552 --> 00:04:21.451
directly inferior to the inferior colliculus,

77
00:04:21.451 --> 00:04:24.608
and directly superior to the reticular formation.

78
00:04:24.608 --> 00:04:30.149
The pons is on the anterior side of the brain stem, directly inferior to the midbrain,

79
00:04:30.149 --> 00:04:35.690
where the inferior colliculus is labelled, directly superior to the medulla oblongata,

80
00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:38.653
and directly anterior to the fourth ventricle.

81
00:04:38.653 --> 00:04:42.583
The medulla oblongata is directly anterior to the cerebellum,

82
00:04:42.583 --> 00:04:44.774
and directly inferior to the pons.

83
00:04:44.774 --> 00:04:50.121
And the corpus callosum is directly superior to the lateral ventricle at the front,

84
00:04:50.121 --> 00:04:53.213
and directly superior to the fornix at the back.

85
00:04:53.213 --> 00:04:56.628
It's inferior to the cingulate gyrus of the cerebrum.

86
00:04:57.078 --> 00:04:59.237
That's thirteen blanks on her test.

87
00:04:59.237 --> 00:05:01.766
Positions are hard to learn by ear alone,

88
00:05:01.766 --> 00:05:05.035
so look at her diagram once with these words playing.

89
00:05:05.485 --> 00:05:06.268
<v Examiner>Once more.

90
00:05:06.268 --> 00:05:06.893
<v Examiner>Case 10.

91
00:05:09.343 --> 00:05:12.300
Area A, the prefrontal cortex: judgement,

92
00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:16.627
so damage brings poor judgement and inappropriate behaviour.

93
00:05:16.627 --> 00:05:19.079
B, Broca's area: producing speech.

94
00:05:19.079 --> 00:05:23.622
C, the motor area, on the precentral gyrus: voluntary movement.

95
00:05:23.622 --> 00:05:27.877
D, the somatosensory area, on the postcentral gyrus: touch.

96
00:05:27.877 --> 00:05:30.040
E, the auditory area: hearing.

97
00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:33.141
F, Wernicke's area: understanding language.

98
00:05:33.141 --> 00:05:35.305
G, the occipital area: vision.

99
00:05:35.305 --> 00:05:37.324
Lose the area, lose its job.

100
00:05:37.324 --> 00:05:40.064
Drunk with no alcohol: the cerebellum.

101
00:05:40.064 --> 00:05:42.588
Long, gasping in-breaths: the pons.

102
00:05:42.588 --> 00:05:46.338
Very slow, shallow breathing: the medulla oblongata.

103
00:05:46.338 --> 00:05:49.800
Hands acting independently: the corpus callosum.
